<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xuzhou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pengcheng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pengcheng was the ancient name of Xuzhou in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A key crossroads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>'s Western Chu after 206 BCE, and in 205 BCE Xiang Yu routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>'s much larger army there in the Battle of Pengcheng. It remained a strategic city in later wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuzhou.jpg" alt="Xuzhou" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sprt98 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuzhou_City_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Xuzhou, also known as Pengcheng, is a prefecture-level city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> province. It lies at the junction of four provinces, Jiangsu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and Anhui, in a natural gap between the Shandong Hills and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. It stands at the south-eastern end of the North China Plain, on deep alluvial deposits from the migrations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, and limestone hills rise abruptly from the floodplain, the highest of them Dadong Mountain at 361 metres. The city is the ancestral home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> imperial family and is rich in Han archaeological remains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological remains in the region are largely associated with the Dawenkou culture. In the Shang dynasty a polity called Dapeng, or Peng, associated with the Dongyi, dominated the area, and it was subordinated to Shang authority after the campaigns of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-ding">Wu Ding</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification the region was organised as a commandery. During the collapse of Qin, Pengcheng became a centre of Chu revival: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yi-of-chu">Emperor Yi of Chu</a> moved his seat there in 208 BCE, and in 206 BCE Xiang Yu made it the capital of Western Chu. Despite his victory at the Battle of Pengcheng in 205 BCE the city passed to Han control, and in 202 BCE it became the capital of the Chu Princedom under Liu Jiao.</p><p>In 154 BCE Prince Liu Wu joined the Rebellion of the Seven Princes. After his defeat his Shizishan tomb nevertheless exceeded the usual limits and contained gold-threaded jade burial suits. After the Revolution of 1911 the railways completed in 1912 and 1915 made Xuzhou a junction and a recurring target for rival factions. General Zhang Xun used it as a base and held four Xuzhou Conferences in 1916 and 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Xuzhou had about 9.08 million inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> industry began with the Jiawang Coal Mine in 1882, and for decades that was the only industrial-scale coal extraction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>. Most local mines have since closed, and the city has become a centre of heavy machinery manufacturing. It is a major rail hub where the Beijing-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> line meets the east-west Land Bridge corridor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xuzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 466–467 the Song governors of Xu and Yan provinces surrendered the populous lands around <b>Pengcheng</b> to the Wei.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 206 BCE Liu Bang conquered the Three Qins, then fought Western Chu for four years, losing at <b>Pengcheng</b> but recovering.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-han-206-bc">Kingdom of Han (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>Its loss opened the route east for Liu Bang's ill-fated march on <b>Pengcheng</b>, capital of Western Chu.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-henan">Kingdom of Henan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 206 BCE he divided the empire into eighteen kingdoms and took for himself the title of Hegemon-King of Western Chu, ruling from <b>Pengcheng</b> (Xuzhou).</blockquote><a href="/state/western-chu">Western Chu</a></li><li><blockquote>Wei Bao joined Liu Bang in 205 BCE, then deserted him after the Han defeat at <b>Pengcheng</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-wei-206-bc">Western Wei (206 BC)</a></li></ul></section>
